$ git show HEAD:tools/pi-trim-result.mjs | grep -c 'repeatRefus'
0
$ git show HEAD:tools/pi-child-events.sh | grep -c 'LOOP_REPEAT_READ_REFUSALS'
0
$ git show HEAD:tools/loop/loop.conf | grep -c 'LOOP_REPEAT_READ_REFUSALS'
0

The base has neither the key, the export nor the refusal: `repeatReadResult` answers a repeat by the arm only, and
nothing counts the repeats of one path. The case is run against the base's own configuration — every file the tree
edits is in place, only the recorded key is HEAD's — so the run below isolates what the change owes:

$ git show HEAD:tools/loop/loop.conf > /tmp/red989/head-loop.conf
$ wc -l < /tmp/red989/head-loop.conf
1090
$ grep -c 'LOOP_REPEAT_READ_REFUSALS' /tmp/red989/head-loop.conf
0                                    # grep exits 1: no such line

$ LOOP_CONF=/tmp/red989/head-loop.conf bash tools/loop/tests/loop-selftest.sh \
    --case adapter#aPathIsRefusedAfterItsRepeatBound
adapter#aPathIsRefusedAfterItsRepeatBound|   loop.conf records nothing for LOOP_REPEAT_READ_REFUSALS, so one path may be asked for without end (issue #989)
--- adapter#aPathIsRefusedAfterItsRepeatBound[0] (exit 1)
  loop.conf records nothing for LOOP_REPEAT_READ_REFUSALS, so one path may be asked for without end (issue #989)
FAIL adapter#aPathIsRefusedAfterItsRepeatBound
loop-selftest: 1 of 1 cases failed

RED on the base for the reason the case exists: with no count bound recorded, a path may be asked for without end and
each re-ask is a turn (#943: 2 372 reads of one gate log in 245 turns; #872: four paths at 283 to 295 reads each across
226 turns). `loop-cases.sh --case <name>` prints nothing and exits 0 — the dispatch lives in the suite, so the evidence
runs the suite's own `--case` entry.